-- title:
--   Exponent shift under the inverse Frobenius re-basing: n_{t'}=nₜ+c
-- statement:
--   The setting is fixed by the following groups of data and hypotheses.
--
--   **Residue characteristic and level.** Primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$ (`hrr`), and a non-zero natural number $N$ with $r \nmid N$ (`hrN`).
--
--   **Coefficient rings.** A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible element $\pi \in \mathcal O$ (`hπ`) such that $\mathcal O$ is $(\pi)$-adically complete (`hcomplete`), the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements (`hres`), and $(r) = (\pi)$ as ideals of $\mathcal O$ (`hunr`); a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **The unramified extension and its Frobenius.** A characteristic-zero domain $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra, together with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, subject to: $(\pi)$-adic completeness of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ (`hOnr_complete`), maximality of the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$ (`hOnr_max`), integrality of every element over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), the existence modulo $\pi$ of a root of every monic polynomial of positive degree (`hOnr_closed`), and the congruence $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^{r} \pmod{\pi}$ for all $x$ (`hFr`).
--
--   **Quaternionic data.** Rationals $a,b$ such that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ is indefinite ($0<a$ or $0<b$) and the finite places at which it is a division algebra are exactly those above $r$ and $\bar r$ (`hB`); a $\mathbb Z$-submodule $\Lambda \subseteq \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ which is a maximal order (`hΛ`) and contains every rational integer (`hΛℤ`); a map $\mathrm{coord} : \Lambda \to \mathbb Z_{r}^{(2)} \times \mathbb Z_{r}^{(2)}$ (where $\mathbb Z_{r}^{(2)} =$ `Zp2 r` is the ring of Witt vectors of the field with $r^{2}$ elements) which is additive, sends $1$ to $(1,0)$, satisfies the Frobenius-twisted multiplication rule of `IsOrderCoord`, is injective, has dense image in each $r$-adic truncation, and matches reduced traces with the first coordinate plus its Frobenius (`hcoord`).
--
--   **Data on the special fibre.** A fake elliptic curve $A_0$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$ for $\Lambda$ and level $N$ (an abelian scheme datum with commutative relative group law, two-dimensional fibres, a $\Lambda$-action compatible with the group law and with the trace condition, and level structure); a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$ of height $4$, i.e. $[r]$ has kernel of degree $r^{4}$ (`hX₀`); formal coordinates $\theta_0$ in two variables for $A_0$, such that $\theta_0$ realises $X_0$ as the formal module of $A_0$ through $\mathrm{coord}$ (`hA₀`).
--
--   **The Drinfeld-side moduli data.** A ring homomorphism $\iota : \mathbb Z_{r}^{(2)} \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}$; a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ which is special for the reduction of $\iota$ (`hΦ`, i.e. its two Lie pieces are complementary and invertible) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`); a moduli package $M$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ which is a Zariski sheaf (`hM`); a family $\eta$ assigning to each rigidified object $t = (X,n,\rho)$ over a ring $B$ with $r$ nilpotent and each $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$-structure $\psi$ a point of $M$; and the three clauses of `hη`: on Noetherian bases, $\eta$ separates admissible rigidified objects exactly up to the isomorphism relation `Rigidified.IsIsomorphic`; $\eta$ is compatible with base change of admissible objects; and every point of $M$ is, locally on a Zariski cover by localisations, of the form $\eta(t)$ for an admissible $t$. Finally, a ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of the action endomorphisms of $\Phi$ together with $\varpi_\Phi$ into $M_2(K_0)$, with `hE₀` asserting that $E_0$ is injective and that for some $m$ the image of $E_0$ contains $r^{m}M_2(\mathcal O)$ and is contained in $r^{-m}M_2(\mathcal O)$.
--
--   **Comparison of the two reductions.** A ring homomorphism $\kappa : O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi) \to O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ compatible with the two quotient maps (`hκ`); a natural number $n_0$ and a two-variable series $\beta_0$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ which is an $\mathcal O_D$-homomorphism $\Phi \to X_0 \otimes_\kappa O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(r)$ with kernel of degree $r^{4n_0}$ (`hβ₀`).
--
--   **The supersingular dictionary.** A family $\Xi$ assigning to each Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$, each $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent in $B$ and each rigidified curve over $(B,\psi)$ a $G$-point of $M$ over $B$, subject to four laws: `hΞleg`, that over a connected base the leg of $\Xi$ is $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{k}$ for some $k \in \mathbb Z$; `hΞnat`, that $\Xi$ commutes with base change along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps for compatible pullbacks of the curve and of its rigidification; `hΞiso` (the hypothesis is a single implication with the auxiliary comparison data summarised here), that $\Xi$ takes the same value on two rigidified curves over $(B,\psi)$ whose underlying curves are isomorphic compatibly with group law, $\Lambda$-action and level, provided the rigidifications are matched by maps of the reductions agreeing with the isogenies up to multiplication by powers of $r$; and `hΞdef`, that over a connected base with $r$ nilpotent, for every rigidified curve $x$ with formal module structure $(X,\theta)$ there are $j \le 1$ and a rigidified object $t$ with $t.X = X$, with $t$ a rigidification transport of $x$ of parity $j$ relative to $(\theta_0,\kappa,\beta_0)$, admissible for $\iota$ and the leg $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-j}$, such that $\Xi(x) = \langle \psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-j}, \eta(t)\rangle$.
--
--   **The inverse Frobenius twist of $A_0$.** A fake elliptic curve $A_{0}^{r}$ over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$ and a morphism $\mathrm{pr}_A : A_{0}^{r}.A \to A_0.A$ exhibiting $A_{0}^{r}$ as the pullback of $A_0$ along the map induced by $\mathrm{Fr}^{-1}$ on $O^{\mathrm{nr}}/(\pi)$ (`hprA`), together with morphisms $F : A_0.A \to A_{0}^{r}.A$ and $V : A_{0}^{r}.A \to A_0.A$ over the base (`hF`, `hV`) which are homomorphisms for the relative group laws (`F_hom`, `V_hom`), commute with the $\Lambda$-actions (`F_act`, `V_act`), preserve the level structures (`F_lev`, `V_lev`), satisfy $V \circ F = [r]$ on $A_0$ and $F \circ V = [r]$ on $A_{0}^{r}$ on points (`V_F`, `F_V`), and such that on points with values in a ring of characteristic $r$ the composite of $V$ with $\mathrm{pr}_A$ is the $r$-power Frobenius twist of the point (`V_frob`).
--
--   **The base and the two rigidifications.** A non-trivial Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\mathcal O$-algebra map $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$, the image of $\pi$ nilpotent in $B$ (`hBπ`), and $B$ connected in the sense that every idempotent is $0$ or $1$ (`hconn`); a fake elliptic curve $E$ over $B$; a rigidification $\rho$ of $E$ relative to $A_0$ and $\psi$, and a rigidification $\rho^{-}$ of the same $E$ relative to $A_0$ and the re-based leg $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-1}$ (each consisting of the reduction $E_b$ of $E$ modulo $\pi$, a pullback $A_b$ of $A_0$ along the residue leg, a natural number $d$, and an isogeny pair $\varphi,\varphi'$ of degree $r^{d}$ between $E_b$ and $A_b$ preserving level). These are pinned together by: $ub : \rho.E_b.A \to \rho^{-}.E_b.A$ over the base with $ub$ followed by $\rho^{-}.g_b$ equal to $\rho.g_b$ (`hub`, `hub'`); a morphism $g_A' : \rho^{-}.A_b.A \to A_{0}^{r}.A$ exhibiting $\rho^{-}.A_b$ as the pullback of $A_{0}^{r}$ along the residue leg of $\psi$ (`hgA'`) with $g_A'$ followed by $\mathrm{pr}_A$ equal to $\rho^{-}.g_A$ (`hgA''`); morphisms $F_b : \rho.A_b.A \to \rho^{-}.A_b.A$ and $V_b : \rho^{-}.A_b.A \to \rho.A_b.A$ over the base lifting $F$ and $V$ (`hFb`, `hFb'`, `hVb`, `hVb'`); the degree shift $\rho^{-}.d = \rho.d + 1$ (`hd`); and the compatibilities $ub$ followed by $\rho^{-}.\varphi$ equals $\rho.\varphi$ followed by $F_b$ (`hφ`) and $\rho^{-}.\varphi' = V_b$ followed by $\rho.\varphi'$ followed by $ub$ (`hφ'`).
--
--   **The two transports.** The image of $r$ is nilpotent in $B$ (`hBr`). A formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X$ over $B$ and formal coordinates $\theta$ for $E$ realising $X$ through $\mathrm{coord}$ (`hX`), a parity $j \le 1$ and a rigidified object $t = (X_t, n_t, \rho_t)$ over $B$ with $X_t = X$ (`htX`), with $t$ a rigidification transport of $\rho$ in the coordinates $\theta$ of parity $j$ relative to $(\theta_0,\kappa,\beta_0)$ (`htr`: there are a reduction map $\kappa_B$ and a series $\sigma$ over $B/(\pi)$ compatible with the quotient and residue maps, transporting $\theta_0$ through $\rho.\varphi'$ to $\theta$, and $\rho_t$ is the composite of $\sigma$ reduced by $\kappa_B$ with $\beta_0$ reduced along $\psi$ and with $X_i \mapsto X_i^{r^{j}}$), and $t$ admissible for $\iota$ and the leg $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-j}$ (`hadm`: $X_t$ special, of height $4$, and $\rho_t$ an isogeny of height $4n_t$ from the reduction of $\Phi$ to that of $X_t$). Likewise $X'$, $\theta'$ with `hX'`, a parity $j' \le 1$ and a rigidified object $t' = (X_{t'}, n_{t'}, \rho_{t'})$ with $X_{t'} = X'$ (`ht'X`), which is a transport of $\rho^{-}$ in the coordinates $\theta'$ of parity $j'$ (`htr'`) and is admissible for $\iota$ and the leg $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-1} \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-j'}$ (`hadm'`).
--
--   **The exponent.** An integer $c$ such that, with $Z \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$ the scalar matrix attached to the unit $r$ of $K_0$, the hypothesis `hact` holds: the dictionary point $\Xi(E,\rho^{-})$ over the leg $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-1}$ is obtained from $\Xi(E,\rho)$ by the action of $Z^{c}$ in the sense of `ModuliPackage.G.IsActBy`, namely there exist an element $e$ of the centraliser of the action endomorphisms of $\Phi$ and $\varpi_\Phi$, and natural numbers $k$ and $m'$, with $E_0(e) = r^{k}\,(Z^{c})^{-1}$, with the power series of $e$ having kernel of degree $r^{2m'}$, and with the predicate `IsTranslate` holding for $\iota$, $\Phi$, $\eta$, $\mathrm{Fr}$, the series of $e$, $k$, $m'$ and the two points.
--
--   Under these hypotheses the conclusion is the single equality of integers
--   $$n_{t'} = n_{t} + c,$$
--   that is, the height exponents recorded by the two rigidified objects satisfy $(t'.n : \mathbb Z) = (t.n : \mathbb Z) + c$.
--
--   This is the bookkeeping step in the Čerednik–Drinfeld comparison which records how the height exponent of a Drinfeld-side rigidified object changes when a rigidification of a fake elliptic curve is re-based along the inverse Frobenius twist of its reduction: the shift of the exponent is exactly the integer $c$ measuring the displacement of the corresponding points of the moduli package under the central element $r \cdot 1 \in \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0)$. It is used in the passage to full level structure, in [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.FakeEllipticCurve.Rigidification.fullLevel_eq_of_isNormLevelTransport_of_isNormLevelTransport_frobTwist_neg_one_of_isActBy`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.FakeEllipticCurve.Rigidification.fullLevel_eq_of_isNormLevelTransport_of_isNormLevelTransport_frobTwist_neg_one_of_isActBy).
